<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Comayagua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Comayagua is a city in central Honduras, founded in 1537 by the Spanish. As the seat of the colonial governors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> it was the capital of Honduras, and after independence it alternated with its rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> as the seat of government until 1880, when the capital moved permanently to Tegucigalpa. Its colonial cathedral and churches survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/comayagua.jpg" alt="Comayagua" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luis Alfredo Romero · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_de_Comayagua_collague.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Comayagua lies in a valley 80 km north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> on the highway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-pedro-sula">San Pedro Sula</a>, 594 metres above sea level, and is the capital of the Comayagua department. Hondurans call it La Antañona, the old one, because it keeps much of its colonial architecture. The name is indigenous: most agree it combines the Lenca words koma, a huge amount of land, and jawa, water, meaning abundant land of water. The Spaniards called the place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and the Country of the Fig Trees. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenca-people">Lenca people</a> have lived in the valley since approximately 1,000 BCE, as remains at Yarumela and Tenampúa show.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Captain Alonso de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a> founded the city in 1537, on the instructions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a>, to find a site for a city between the two oceans. It was first called Santa María de la Concepción de Comayagua. King Philip II granted it the title of city in 1557, and in 1561 the episcopal see moved there from Trujillo because of its central position and its closeness to the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining regions. It remained the capital of Honduras throughout the colonial period, and in 1788 it became an intendancy that absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, which had grown as a mining centre. This fed a rivalry between the two cities.</p><p>Honduras became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">United Provinces of Central America</a> in 1823, and Comayagua stayed its capital. On 4 April 1827 federal troops under Colonel Justo Milla laid siege to the city to overthrow the head of state, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionisio-de-herrera">Dionisio de Herrera</a>, and much of it was burned and looted. A decree of June 1849 to move the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> was not carried out. On 30 October 1880 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a> decided to transfer it, after which the population, commerce and importance of Comayagua fell notably, though it remained the seat of the diocese of Honduras.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Comayagua has some of the oldest buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, among them the churches of La Merced, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> and San Sebastián, built in the 16th century. The cathedral of the Immaculate Conception, built in the colonial period, holds a clock that is said to be the oldest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The Caxa Real, built between 1739 and 1741, stored the tribute and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold of the Spanish crown. Museums cover colonial religious art, the Lenca and the republican period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Comayagua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In colonial times Honduras was a thinly settled province whose silver mines around Tegucigalpa and cattle ranches supported a small creole elite, while its governor and bishop resided at <b>Comayagua</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The former province of <b>Comayagua</b> and the mining district of Tegucigalpa formed the State of Honduras within the Federal Republic of Central America.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-honduras">State of Honduras</a></li></ul></section>
